## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design a scalable, multi-tenant data ingestion architecture across AWS accounts while addressing security, reliability, and cost optimization challenges.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account IAM roles with trust relationships
- Tenant isolation through data partitioning and access controls
- Exactly-once processing using idempotent operations
- Kinesis Data Streams, Lambda, Firehose, and S3 integration
- KMS encryption and data retention policies
- Cost management through throttling and lifecycle strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Idempotent processor using DynamoDB for deduplication
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const dynamodb = new AWS.DynamoDB.DocumentClient();

exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const failures = [];

  for (const record of event.Records) {
    const { tenantId, sequenceId, data } = JSON.parse(record.kinesis.data);
    const tableName = process.env.DDB_TABLE;

    try {
      // Check if sequence already processed
      const result = await dynamodb.get({
        TableName: tableName,
        Key: { tenantId, sequenceId }
      }).promise();

      if (result.Item) {
        continue; // Skip duplicate
      }

      // Process data...
      await processData(tenantId, data);

      // Mark as processed
      await dynamodb.put({
        TableName: tableName,
        Item: { tenantId, sequenceId, processedAt: Date.now() }
      }).promise();

    } catch (error) {
      failures.push({ itemIdentifier: record.kinesis.sequenceNumber });
    }
  }

  return { batchItemFailures: failures };
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests cross-account access, tenant isolation, and end-to-end data export controls in a realistic setup.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-account roles and STS AssumeRole with session tags
- S3 prefixes and object-level permissions
- TLS enforcement and per-tenant KMS keys
- CloudTrail auditing of cross-account activity

## Code Example

```json
// Trust policy for the tenant-scoped ExportRole
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::DATA-LAKE-ACCOUNT:role/ExporterRole" },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": { "StringEquals": { "aws:RequestTag/tenant_id": "tenant-123" } }
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
// Tenant bucket policy sample for restricted CopyObject
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:PutObject","s3:PutObjectAcl","s3:GetObject","s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": ["arn:aws:s3:::tenant-bucket-123","arn:aws:s3:::tenant-bucket-123/*"],
      "Condition": { "StringEquals": { "s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption": "aws:kms" } }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this to dozens/hundreds of tenants?
- What are failure modes if the tenant account lacks KMS keys or if the ExportRole permissions are too permissive?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical AWS multi-tenant isolation at scale, combining IAM session tags, Lake Formation, data catalog usage, and automated revocation; probes policy design, GitOps deployment, and testing.

## Key Concepts
- IAM role session tags
- Lake Formation permissions
- Data catalog integration
- Event-driven revocation with DynamoDB, EventBridge, Step Functions
- GitOps deployment of IAM policies
- Auditability: CloudTrail, Config, Access Analyzer

## Code Example
```json
// Trust policy snippet (AssumeRole requires matching tenant-id tag)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"
        }
      },
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/PrincipalRoleForTenant"}
    }
  ]
}
```
```json
// Permission policy snippet (tenant-scoped S3 and Lake Formation actions)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject","s3:ListBucket"],
      "Resource": [
        "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-data/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}/*",
        "arn:aws:s3:::tenant-data/${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"
      ]
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["lakeformation:GetDataAccess"],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"lakeformation:PrincipalTag/tenant-id": "${aws:PrincipalTag/tenant-id}"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-tenant leakage with automated CI checks?
- What monitoring would you add to detect revocation failures and alert on latency breaches?
